\DOC BOUNDER_TAC

\TYPE {BOUNDER_TAC : thm list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
{BOUNDER_TAC} closes a goal asserting numeric bounds by automatic interval
reasoning.

\DESCRIBE
Takes one argument of type {thm list}, a list of theorems supplied alongside
the goal; in all observed successes this list is empty, while failures occur
both with an empty list and with a long list of theorems drawn from the ambient
context. The goal's conclusion {t} is a bound assertion ({u < v}, {u <= v}, or
a conjunction of two such, with either side an arithmetic expression) over
naturals, integers or reals built from word values, bignum expressions,
arithmetic and numerals. The assumptions {A} are left untouched and are not
needed, however large and heterogeneous they are.
{
          A ?- t
     ====================  BOUNDER_TAC [] 
         (no subgoals)
}

\FAILURE
Fails when the conclusion is not a bound assertion that its interval analysis
can verify: observed failures are on an equation between opaque terms and on a
negated equation, raising a rewriting-level error, and on a conjunction of
inequalities over integer-division expressions whose bounds it cannot
establish, reporting that no tactic applied. Since these failures arise with
both an empty and a populated theorem-list argument, they stem from the
tactic's own inability to handle the conclusion rather than from the supplied
theorems.

\COMMENTS
In the observed traces, every goal with a bound-assertion conclusion was
closed, and no trace shows the tactic transforming a goal rather than closing
it.

\ENDDOC
